ible trade  agreement it can help  restore trust but we are hearing from  politicians in the european parliament  that they are  not sure about that that damage has been  done that they don't know if they can  trust  the uk and their government and  of course some voices here are also  saying that it could be an excuse for  boris johnson to say okay  we now showed this uh gesture of  goodwill and if the eu  is not ready to compromise that we can  just walk away so i would say that  it can go both ways still  can go both ways dw brussels bureau  chief alexander vanaman reporting  thank you now the two sides need to come  to an agreement before  january 1st otherwise from that day  onward  tariffs will have to be paid on goods  entering the uk from the eu  and the other way around so what's at  stake well the world trade organization  puts the figure at 3.1 percent for  customs tariffs for goods from the eu to  the uk  and 3.3 percent for goods heading the  other way  that's not all certain types of products  could even have much higher duty  much higher duties rather come the new  year cars in agriculture could see  tariffs as high as 13 while products  like milk  could even see something as whopping as  36 percent  now with the economies on both sides of  the channel suffering due to the corona  virus  crisis neither wants brexit to happen  without a deal in place  now i'm joined now by ulrich ackermann  he's head of foreign trade for germany's  vdma mechanical engineering association  hello mr ackermann thank you for joining  us hello  i wanted to ask you um how are your  members approaching this moment right  now as the negotiations near  yeah the member companies have to adapt  to a hard brexit or to a smooth brexit  that means they have to adapt their  international i.t processes  they have to prepare the relevant custom  documents  and if a hard brexit comes they have to  pay custom duties  but what is open now is how will  the impact of the border controls be 